! <<lingo Name>>

<<lingo Description>>

This is a low-level JS library for developing plugins that will generate activity logs.

The following is a description of the development tools provided with this plug-in.

!! Creating Log Files

!!! JS Library

!!!! createActivityLog

Creates a log file, if it already exists it will be overwritten depending on the options.

```ts
function createActivityLog(logTiddlerType: LogFileTypes, fields: Partial<ITiddlerFields> & Pick<ITiddlerFields, 'title'>, options?: {
    overwrite?: boolean;
}): void
```

Usage

```ts
import { createActivityLog } from '$:/plugins/linonetwo/activity-log-tools/log-file-tools/createActivityLog';
createActivityLog(activityLog.type, { title: activityLog.title });
```

!!!! addActivityToLog

Adds an activity log to the log file, if it doesn't exist it will be created for you.

```ts
function addActivityToLog(activityLog: IActivityLogFile | undefined, newActivityTimestamp: number): void
```

Usage

```ts
import { addActivityToLog } from '$:/plugins/linonetwo/activity-log-tools/log-file-tools/addActivityToLog';
addActivityToLog(eventLog, event.timestamp)
```

!!! Action Widgets (WikiText)

If your plugin is mostly written in WikiText, you can use these action widgets directly:

!!!! action-create-activity-log

Create a log tiddler.

```tid
<$action-create-activity-log
    $logTitle=<<logTitle>>
    $logType="daily-count"
/>
```

!!!! action-add-activity-to-log

Add one activity record into a log file.

```tid
<$action-add-activity-to-log
    $logTitle=<<logTitle>>
    $logType="daily-count"
/>
```

!! Read the log file

!!! JS Library

!!!! getActivityLog

Gets a log file based on the provided header, see the `IActivityLogFile` type, and the contents of the log will be stored in an ES6 `Map`, depending on the specific type of the file. The second parameter provided is not actually used, but just added directly to the returned object. If the tiddler doesn't exist, an empty `Map` is returned, which you can use as usual to add new stuff to, and then create the corresponding tiddler when storing it.

```ts
function getActivityLog(logTiddlerTitle: string, logTiddlerType: LogFileTypes): IActivityLogFile | undefined
```

Usage

```ts
import { getActivityLog } from '$:/plugins/linonetwo/activity-log-tools/log-file-tools/getActivityLog';
const activityLog = getActivityLog(title, logTiddlerType);
```

!!! Filters

You can use filters to get summaries of logs for visualization and other scenarios.

!!!! activitydaycounts

Calculates the number of activities per day for a given time frame. (The following example uses the `weekstart` and `weekend` macros from the [[visualization dashboard plugin|https://tw-cpl.netlify.app/#Plugin_202403161108223]])

```scss
[[logFileTitle]activitydaycounts<weekstart>,<weekend>[]]
```


!! Check-ins in a time range

`activityevents<start>,<end>` returns one JSON string per check-in, or per day for daily-count formats. Each record contains `title`, `timestamp` (UTC TiddlyWiki date), `start`, `allDay`, and `extendedProps.activityCount`. The range includes its start and excludes its end. Use local `YYYY-MM-DD` dates, ISO dates with an offset, or UTC TiddlyWiki dates as bounds.

* `activityevents:titles<start>,<end>`: titles of logs containing check-ins in the range.
* `activityevents:timestamps<start>,<end>`: check-in timestamps; daily counts use the start of their local day.
* `activityevents<start>,<end>`: event JSON records, keeping each title with its timestamp and count.

```wikitext
<$let start="2026-11-01" end="2026-12-01" events={{{ [tag[$:/Tags/ActivityLog]activityevents<start>,<end>] }}}>
<$list filter="[(events)]" variable="event">
<$text text={{{ [<event>jsonget[title]] }}}/> · <$text text={{{ [<event>jsonget[timestamp]] }}}/><br/>
</$list>
</$let>
```

Multi-valued variables require TiddlyWiki 5.4.0. Each event remains a single JSON string because filters return lists of strings.

With the Calendar plugin's `eventFilter` support:

```wikitext
<$calendar filter="[all[]limit[0]]" eventFilter="[tag[$:/Tags/ActivityLog]activityevents<calendarStart>,<calendarEnd>]" />
```

Timestamp and interval formats retain exact times. Daily counts and RLE display all-day counts. Date-only history remains all-day after conversion, while new timed check-ins appear at their recorded times. `activity-log-type` identifies the storage format; `activity-log-day-counts` stores date-only counts in timestamp and interval logs. The event index uses the core's tiddler cache and is invalidated when the log changes; daily counts are not expanded into individual events.

Conversion undo is held in memory until the page is reloaded; no backup tiddler is saved.
